GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/codex_t19p1.txt:27:**READ:** Missing census entries include Malta’s GT timer on ISA9 (`machine_evbmips.c`, GT setup; `dev_gt.c`, `DEVICE_TICK(gt)`), which repeatedly asserts and has its own read acknowledgement. LPT connects ISA7 but has no implemented assertion; the optional floppy is likewise a dummy without assertion calls. VGA initialization supplies no IRQ.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/codex_t19p1.txt-28-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/codex_t19p1.txt:29:**READ:** `interrupt.h`, `INTERRUPT_ASSERT`, invokes the callback unconditionally. It neither remembers a level nor suppresses repeated assertions. A callback invocation therefore cannot automatically be interpreted as a new electrical edge.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/codex_t19p1.txt-30-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/codex_t19p1.txt-31-**READ:** The PIT has no normal pending-event retirement or deassertion path within `dev_8253.c`. Moreover, there is an additional accounting defect: `bus_isa_init` captures `machine->isa_pic_data.pending_timer_interrupts` **before** creating the PIT; `DEVINIT(8253)` publishes the pointer afterward. `machine_new` zero-initializes the machine, so ordinary Malta initialization leaves the bus’s captured pointer NULL. Algor/V3 has the same ordering problem. The conditional decrement in each ISA deassert handler therefore does not establish working PIT accounting.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/codex_t19p1.txt-32-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/codex_t19p1.txt-33-**READ:** Malta constructs the PIT at ISA port `0x40`; it is reachable. `DEVICE_ACCESS(8253)` sets `in_use` on any access, but an access alone does not guarantee a pending timer event.
