GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-58-**(v) Whether a malta guest programs the PIT. Split.** READ: the comment in `DEVICE_TICK(8253)` says Linux/MALTA kernels want the interrupt regardless of the mode bits. READ, NetBSD current `sys/arch/evbmips/malta/malta_intr.c`: `malta_cal_timer` times the CP0 count from the mc146818, and the clock is that count. `pcib_isa_intr_evcnt` panics on irq 0. NOT CHECKABLE: whether any guest image this fork boots was built with Linux `CONFIG_I8253` (`setup_pit_timer` is under that ifdef in `arch/mips/mti-malta/malta-time.c`) or writes counter 0's MSB. `in_use = 1` on any 8253 access is not enough. The timer is created only on the counter-0 MSB write.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-59-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-60-**2.5 Witness numbers are the packet's measurements.** I did not open `regress/artifacts/`. The probe body is not in `regress/*.py`. Row stimuli I will use are only the ones the packet attaches to a row: `eoi irr4` is specific EOI `0x64` and a read of bit 4 two instructions later; `eoi x8` is that pair eight times (fix `0xff` is eight samples of bit 4, not eight different lines); `nonspec irr4` is EOI `0x20`; `masked irr4` is OCW1 `0x10` with no EOI stated; `poll byte` is OCW3 `0x0c` returning `0x84`.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-61-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt:62:**2.6 Datasheet. READ** (`8259A.txt`). At INTA the highest ISR bit is set and the corresponding IRR bit is reset. EOI resets an ISR bit. Specific EOI is `EOI=1, SL=1, R=0`. Poll's next read is an interrupt acknowledge and sets the IS bit. `LTIM=0`: low-to-high, and the pin may stay high without another interrupt. `LTIM=1`: a high level, and the request must be gone before EOI or the interrupt returns. `fatal()` in this tree only prints (`src/core/debugmsg.c`), so ICW1 bit 3 does not stop the guest and does not switch the model into level mode.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-63-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-64-**2.7 Guests. READ from current NetBSD and Linux sources, not from the packet.**
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-65-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-66-NetBSD `sys/arch/evbmips/malta/pci/pcib.c` `pcib_intr`: write `OCW3_SELECT|OCW3_POLL` (`0x0c`) and read; if the index is 2, poll the slave; call the handlers; then specific EOI. The constants in `sys/dev/ic/i8259reg.h` are `OCW2_EOI = 1<<5`, `OCW2_SL = 1<<6`, `OCW2_ILS(n) = n`, so the store is `0x60 | n` to the slave and then to the master (cascade uses index 2). The GT IACK read is in the `#else` and is not compiled. Init writes ICW1 `ICW1_SELECT|ICW1_IC4` (`0x11`, `LTIM` clear) and OCW3 `0x68` (`OCW3_SELECT|OCW3_SSMM|OCW3_SMM`). `0x68` is the empty "Set Special Mask Mode" arm in `DEVICE_ACCESS(8259)`. NetBSD also writes an ELCR, default 0. Nothing in `src/` implements an ELCR.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-67-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt:68:Linux `arch/mips/mti-malta/malta-int.c` `mips_pcibios_iack` reads `GT_PCI0_IACK` for the GT64120, or the MSC or Bonito equivalent. It does not write OCW3 `0x0c`. One malta revision does `i8259_set_poll(mips_pcibios_iack)`, which replaces the poll function with that read. `GT_PCI0_INTR_ACK` in `dev_gt.c` returns `isa_pic_data.last_int` and has the comment that it does not acknowledge. `mask_and_ack_8259A` in `drivers/irqchip/irq-i8259.c` writes `0x60+(irq&7)` and then `0x60+2` for the cascade. The packet's "malta obtains the vector with OCW3 `0x0c`" is true of NetBSD `pcib_intr` and of the generic `i8259_irq()` poll path. It is false of current Linux malta dispatch.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-69-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt:70:`DEVICE_ACCESS(prep)` returns `last_int` and has no acknowledge side effect. That part of `i8259isrnever` is real.
