GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-89-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-90-Supplied candidates, against the code:
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-91-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-92-- **(A)** deletes both IRR clears. The recalc compares `irr & ~ier` with itself and does not touch the cause bit. The device deassert path still does, through `isa_interrupt_deassert`. For the 8253 there is no deassert, so IRR bit 0 and the cause bit stay set after the first timer fire. For NetBSD `pcib_intr`'s `for (;;)` poll, a bit that survives the specific EOI makes the next poll return the same irq again.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt:93:- **(B)** adds `isr |= bit` on the poll read and gates the cause bit with `~isr`. It does not reset the IRR bit at that read. The datasheet's acknowledge does both. Linux malta never issues the poll, so for that guest (B) is (A). Guests that do poll still see the 8253 again after EOI, because IRR bit 0 is still set and the `~isr` term is gone. The poll scan also ignores `ier`, so the bit it would latch can be a masked line. The non-specific arm still does `d->isr = 0` (every bit, not the highest) once any ISR bit exists.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-94-- **(C)** is the split that keeps a pulse consumed and leaves a level bit alone. The place that would have to know the mode is the OCW2 arm in `dev_8259.c`. `bus_isa.c` does not see the EOI. The same arm serves V3's PICs. "Never deasserts" selects the 8253 and does not select `pckbc`, which deasserts when the queue drains and still will not re-assert while `currently_asserted` stays 1. ICW1 bit 3 is printed and ignored. There is no per-line register in this tree. NetBSD already writes an ELCR that nothing here implements.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-95-- **(D)** restores the bit after the clear if a shadow says the pin is still high. The 8253 never deasserts, so a shadow defined as "asserted and not yet deasserted" restores bit 0 too. The Jazz `isa_int_asserted` mask is this idiom, on a different bridge.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-96-- **(E)** does not change the value `eoi irr4` reads two instructions after `0x64`. The UART already re-asserts every tick. The 8253 already does too. (E) is a `pckbc` change.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-97-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt:98:Missing member, from the ack sites that are actually in `src/`: clear the request at the acknowledge the guest performs, and refill it from a pin shadow only for a level pin. The sites are the poll read (`current_command == 0x0c`, NetBSD malta), `GT_PCI0_INTR_ACK` (Linux malta, today returns `last_int` and does not ack), `DEVICE_ACCESS(prep)` (same, no ack), and the Algor `P5064_LOCINT` / `P5064_ISAINT` reads (already a clear, no refill). (B) covers one site and does not clear IRR there.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-99-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-100-## 4. Scope
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-101-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt:102:Leave `i8259isrnever` as its own row. Same file, different event: ISR is never set, and the PReP and GT IACK reads have no acknowledge side effect. (B) does not fix those reads.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-103-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt:104:With (A) alone on the Linux malta path (GT IACK for the vector, then `mask_and_ack` which masks and writes `0x60+irq`): the mask drops the cause bit, the EOI leaves IRR, and a later unmask asserts again if the bit is still set. For a UART that still has the condition, that is the level behaviour the datasheet describes for `LTIM=1`. Linux programs ICW1 `0x11`, which is `LTIM=0`. For the 8253, if the guest has programmed counter 0 and unmasked irq 0, nothing later clears bit 0, so the unmask asserts again as soon as the handler returns.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-105-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-106-## 5. The 8253
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-107-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt:108:Under (A), nothing clears IRR bit 0. The tick's `INTERRUPT_ASSERT` finds the bit already set, `isa_interrupt_common` sees no change, and it returns without a new cause-bit edge. The existing pulse exists because the specific EOI clears the bit and the next tick sets it again. The count is not the rate. It is a sticky "greater than zero". That stuck count is already a defect (the interrupt rate is the `TICK_SHIFT` 14 tick, and the count never returns to 0). (A) makes it guest-visible: the line stops falling between ticks. The 8253 row in the detector, if its expected value is "bit stays 1", records the stick. It does not reject it.
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-109-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-110-## 6. NetBSD/malta EOI
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt-111-
GXEMUL-SEC/regress/artifacts/i8259eoiirr/pass1_20261025/grok_t19p1.txt:112:**READ**, not from the packet. Specific EOI, `0x60 | n`, in `pcib_intr` in `sys/arch/evbmips/malta/pci/pcib.c`, after a poll acknowledge (`0x0c`) and the handlers. Slave first when `irq > 7`, then the master, with the cascade index forced to 2. `malta_intr.c` itself only delegates to `isa_intr_establish(pcib_ic, ...)`.

 succeeded in 179ms:
  98: /*
  99:  *  v3_isa_interrupt_assert():
 100:  *
 101:  *  Called whenever an ISA device asserts an interrupt (0..15).
 102:  *  If the interrupt number is 16, then it is a re-assert.
 103:  */
 104: void v3_isa_interrupt_assert(struct interrupt *interrupt)
 105: {
 106: 	struct v3_data *d = (struct v3_data *) interrupt->extra;
 107: 	int old_isa_assert, line = interrupt->line;
 108: 	int mask = 1 << (line & 7);
 109: 
 110: 	old_isa_assert = d->pic1->irr & ~d->pic1->ier;
 111: 
 112: 	if (line < 8)
 113: 		d->pic1->irr |= mask;
 114: 	else if (line < 16)
 115: 		d->pic2->irr |= mask;
 116: 
 117: 	v3_isa_interrupt_common(d, old_isa_assert);
 118: }
 119: 
 120: 
 121: /*
 122:  *  v3_isa_interrupt_deassert():
 123:  *
 124:  *  Called whenever an ISA device deasserts an interrupt (0..15).
 125:  *  If the interrupt number is 16, then it is a re-assert.
 126:  */
 127: void v3_isa_interrupt_deassert(struct interrupt *interrupt)
 128: {
 129: 	struct v3_data *d = (struct v3_data *) interrupt->extra;
 130: 	int line = interrupt->line, mask = 1 << (line & 7);
 131: 	int old_irr1 = d->pic1->irr, old_isa_assert;
 132: 
 133: 	old_isa_assert = old_irr1 & ~d->pic1->ier;
 134: 
 135: 	if (line < 8)
 136: 		d->pic1->irr &= ~mask;
 137: 	else if (line < 16)
 138: 		d->pic2->irr &= ~mask;
 139: 
 140: 	/*  If IRQ 0 has been cleared, then this is a timer interrupt.
 141: 	    Let's ack it here:  */
